diff --git a/FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md b/FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md
index f6ec2d4..5e55d0f 100644
--- a/FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md
+++ b/FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md
@@ -138,90 +138,93 @@ no roles and must keep none (`functions/iam-expectations.json`). Don't delete it
   attempt can be attested. **Not attested** (the record says so): folder- and organization-level grants, which
   Christie can't read, and grants on individual Cloud Storage buckets or managed folders, which don't appear on the
   IAM project page (Christie, Sep 30: a known limit, not read).
 - **--reverify <attempt>**: re-reads production and re-probes the **newest** attempt only (production can only speak
   for the attempt that put it there). It carries the attempt's `cli_exit`, backstop and skip-line results forward, so it
   can upgrade only the read-back and the probe. Its record goes under `…/functions-<cb>-verify/`.
 - **A changed guard.** Every tag-writing mode refuses unless the guard running is `origin/main`'s (not dirty, not
   unpushed). If the guard's machinery changed between the attempt and now, `--reconcile`, `--reverify`,
   `--clear-inflight` and `--attest` refuse until Christie OKs it; then re-run with `--acknowledge-verifier-change`, and
   the record says `verifier_changed=yes`. The verdicts are always the tip's code; rebuilding an attempt's expected
   state uses that attempt's own backstop, discovery and hash module (they made what it recorded), and the record names
   that commit as `rebuild_helpers_from`.
 
 ## 8. The pinned FIREBASE_CONFIG
 
 `functions/firebase-config.json` is the body the CLI puts in `FIREBASE_CONFIG` (Firebase's admin-SDK config: project
 id, bucket, location — not a secret). It's pinned rather than fetched, so no script uses the Firebase login (Christie,
 Sep 30), and the expected hash covers it, key order included. The first deploy (D2-5) showed that the live value has
 **no** `locationId` (the guessed `"nam5"` was wrong), so the pin is just `projectId` and `storageBucket`. With it, the
 expected hash equals the live canary's (`5dc5ef93…`).
 If a run reports `FIREBASE_CONFIG differs`, the guard prints the live value: commit it (same key order) in that file,
 get it reviewed, and run the guard again under a new approval.
 
 ## 9. Scheduled functions (D-4)
 
 A scheduled function (`onSchedule`) is declared in `functions/declarations.json` with `"trigger": "schedule"` and its
 whole schedule — the schedule string, `timeZone` (always `"UTC"`) and all five retry values — and **no** `invoker`: the
 CLI always makes the function's own runtime account its only caller and signs the Scheduler's request as it (J3). The
 guard refuses a manifest whose schedule or any retry value differs from the declaration, or is unset (the CLI never
 clears a retry setting removed from code, J5). Plan: `~/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export.html`.
 
 - **The Scheduler job can only be read in the Console.** `functions:list` shows a scheduled function as
   `scheduleTrigger: {}` and nothing else (J1). So a deploy of a codebase with a schedule **always** needs Christie's
   reading (`attest_needed` says "the codebase has a schedule"): Cloud Scheduler → region `us-central1` → the **complete**
   list of job names, and, for each of the codebase's scheduled functions, its job's name
   (`firebase-schedule-<id>-us-central1`, the id's case kept), state, schedule, time zone, target URL, HTTP method, the
   OIDC service account and audience, body, headers, attempt deadline and the five retry values. **Every duration is a
   whole number of seconds** (30m → 1800, 10m → 600, 1h → 3600), counts are numbers, never text. The deploy prints the
   exact shape. `--attest` records `scheduler_attested=yes|no|n/a`; `--status` shows it (`unknown (recorded before D-4)`
   for an attest record written before D-4). It proves what the Console showed then, not later drift; the vault export's
   8-day freshness alert is what catches a job that stops running. Headers: Cloud Scheduler itself adds
   `User-Agent: Google-Cloud-Scheduler`; read every header shown — that one is expected, any other is a finding. A job
   with no OIDC service account can't be written in the reading's shape: that is itself a finding.
 - **The attest record format.** D-4's guard writes `format=tinker-functions-attest-2`, which always has a
   `scheduler_attested` line. A format-1 record (before D-4) without it reads as `unknown (recorded before D-4)`; a
   format-2 record without it is corrupted and `--status` refuses (exit 19) — sort it out by hand, as §4 does for a stray
   tag, with Christie's approval.
 - **A declaration from before D-4** (exactly `serviceAccount`, `invoker`, `ingress`, no `trigger`) is read as an HTTP
   one only when `--reverify` / `--reconcile` rebuild an attempt made then; a new deploy must declare its `trigger`.
 - **The expected job list uses each other codebase's newest attempt, verified or not.** If such an attempt failed
   after dropping a schedule from its declarations, its job (still in Cloud Scheduler) shows up as an extra job and
   every codebase's reading comes back `no` until that codebase is deployed cleanly. It can never give a false `yes`.
 - **Stopping a scheduled function: PAUSE its job** (Cloud Scheduler → the job → Pause). It is immediate and
   reversible (Resume), and changes nothing the CLI reads, so a deploy still verifies. The Scheduler reading does see it:
   a PAUSED job makes `scheduler_attested=no` until it's resumed and read again. The guard never deletes a function or a
   job. Pause first whenever a Scheduler reading comes back `no`, then diagnose.
 - **Partial states** (the CLI creates or patches the job *after* the function):
   - the function is live but its job is missing (the job's creation failed; the deploy exits non-zero): fix the cause,
     then re-run the same sha under the same approval — the job is created then;
   - the job kept its **old** schedule or retry settings (the function updated, the job's patch failed): the Scheduler
     reading shows it (`no`); pause the job, then re-run the same sha;
   - a schedule **removed from code** leaves its job behind, still calling the function. The deploy itself can't see
     the job; the next Scheduler reading does (an extra job in the complete list → `scheduler_attested=no`). So before
     such a change, pause the job, and ask Christie: deleting a job has no guarded path.
 - **The header exception (Christie, Oct 1, O6):** a scheduled function's HTTP response is written by firebase-functions
   itself, so it cannot carry `x-tinker-reached`. The unauthenticated probe still runs on its `uri` and must be refused
   (a public scheduled function would also start a real run). Nothing in the code can make a schedule public: the
   pinned CLI sets its `run.invoker` to the runtime account (J3), and the probe and the IAM reading check the result.
 
 ## 10. The vault codebase (D-4)
 
 `vault/vaultExport` copies the whole database to `gs://my-clay-hub-vault-exports/weekly/<date>-<6 chars>/` in
 `tinker-hq-vault` every Sunday 09:00 UTC, and waits for Google to finish. `vault/vaultFresh` answers an uptime check: 200
 while the newest complete copy is at most 7 days 18 hours old. Plan: `~/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export.html`.
 
 - **To stop exports at once:** Cloud Scheduler → `firebase-schedule-vaultExport-us-central1` → **Pause** (§9). Never
   delete the function or the job by hand. Pausing means the freshness alert fires in about 8 days; that's expected.
 - **A manual catch-up run:** Cloud Scheduler → the job → **Force run**. Google sends a Force run the job's *next*
   scheduled time (seen Oct 7 2026), so `vaultExport` keys a run whose scheduled time is still in the future by the day
   it actually runs. A weekday catch-up therefore never takes the coming Sunday's key, and Sunday still exports.
+  The header can't tell a Force run's retry from a new Force run, so a retry that crosses UTC midnight (6 PM MDT) is
+  keyed by the new day and starts its own export: at most one extra complete copy, in its own folder (O5). Best
+  started before 20:00 UTC (2 PM MDT), so the run and its retries (up to about 2¾ h) finish the same UTC day.
 - **A failure email** (log-based alert on `vaultexport` errors, V13): read the function's log. Its first line holds the
   run's scheduleTime and key (the date); the error names the operation and the prefix. Scheduler retries three times
   (about 10, 20, 40 minutes apart). A retry never starts a second export while the first is visible: it finds the
   complete folder ("already done") or waits on the running export ("resume").
 - **A freshness email** (V9): `vaultFresh`'s body says `stale`, `none` or `error`. `error` usually means `vault-watch@`
   can't list the bucket (its `vaultLister` grant on the bucket).
 - **Two grants live in `tinker-hq-vault`, on the vault bucket only:** `vaultLister` (to `vault-export@` and
   `vault-watch@`, V-1) and `firestoreExportWriter` (to the Firestore service agent, V-4). `--attest` reads only
   `my-clay-hub`'s IAM, so their record is V-1/V-4's screenshots and the exports themselves.
 - **Restoring from the vault:** `docs/my-clay-hub/DATA-RESTORE.md`.
diff --git a/docs/my-clay-hub/DECISIONS.md b/docs/my-clay-hub/DECISIONS.md
index 67a1600..558257b 100644
--- a/docs/my-clay-hub/DECISIONS.md
+++ b/docs/my-clay-hub/DECISIONS.md
@@ -1,81 +1,81 @@
 # Decisions log
 
 Why the app works the way it does. **Don't reverse any of these without asking Christie.** Add new decisions at the bottom, with the date.
 
 Rows marked **SUPERSEDED** were replaced by a later decision (the replacement is named). They're kept so the reasoning stays findable. Rows marked **AMENDED** still stand, with the change noted.
 
 ## Original decisions (handoff, Sept 2026)
 
 | # | Decision | Why | Rejected alternative |
 |---|---|---|---|
 | 1 | Weekly 20-hr cap counts **booked** hours | Members need to see what's left *before* booking, and booking is the only moment the limit can be enforced. | Counting checked-in hours: it's only known after the fact, so it's too late to stop anyone. |
 | 2 | Check-in makes a booking real; no check-in by +30 min **releases it and returns the hours** | Keeps the numbers honest and frees busy spots without punishing anyone. | Penalties for no-shows. |
 | 3 | **No required sign-out.** Auto check-out happens at the booked end time; "Still here?" offers an extension | A required sign-out feels like clocking in and out, and people forget. | Manual check-out required. |
 | 4 | "I'm wrapping up" means finishing **on time**, so no hours are returned. Only **Check out early** returns hours | Christie: tapping "wrapping up" means leaving on time, not early. | Returning the 10 minutes left when they tap. |
 | 5 | "Still here?" appears **10 min** before the end, with **30 min / 1 / 1.5 / 2 hr** choices plus a "Book more time" link | Staying longer should be one tap. 5 minutes is too tight when someone's mid-glaze. | Sending members to the Book page to extend. |
 | 6 | Capacity is a **guideline**: booking is always allowed, the real count is shown even when over (e.g., "5 booked for 4"), and another area is suggested | Christie's call. Blocking frustrates members, and the over-count data supports future equipment decisions. | Hard capacity limits. |
 | 7 | Members book **an area, never a specific station**. No station numbers appear anywhere | Skedda let members book specific spots, which led to "I booked this spot" conflicts. There's nothing to point at if no number is shown. | Numbered stations, or a separate standing-wheel category. |
 | 8 | Standing wheels are **part of Wheels** | Avoids ownership over specific wheels. | A separate category. |
 | 9 | Members see **counts, never names**, for both bookings and RSVPs. **AMENDED Sep 27 by #45:** the in-studio front-door tablet shows a first-name list of who's here now and arriving soon. Everywhere else, counts only. | Showing names would reveal each other's schedules. Counts give members everything they need to plan. | A list of who's booked or going. |
 | 10 | **No messaging.** "Notify everyone going" is a one-way notice | Two-way messaging is a big build and the place bugs pile up. Ask Clay Hub covers questions. | An in-app chat or inbox for replies. |
 | 11 | **No email** from the app beyond Firebase sign-in links | It would mean adding a separate email service, which is more setup and more that can break. | Email as a backup for urgent notices. |
 | 12 | **Inbox first, push later.** The inbox works for everyone regardless of push settings. **PARTLY SUPERSEDED Sep 27 by #46:** push to the two *staff* phones (for no-show alerts) comes in Phase 1. Push to *members* stays later (Phase 4), and the inbox still comes first for members. | Push, especially on iPhone, is the fussiest part of the stack. Each phone must allow push itself, and the app can't force it. | Push in Phase 1. |
 | 13 | Inbox keeps **2 weeks**, and the screen says so | Enough to catch missed items, short enough to stay tidy. The activity log is separate and kept forever. | Keeping the inbox forever. |
 | 14 | The unread signal is a **bell count on the Feed plus a dot on the Feed tab**. **AMENDED Sep 27 by #51:** the dot is **orange** (pink is reserved for cancelled/changes, DESIGN.md). | Visible from every screen without adding a sixth tab. | A Notifications tab. |
 | 15 | Missed-check-in notices are **one card that updates in place** (reminder → last call → released) | Avoids stacking three notices for one booking. | Three separate notifications. |
 | 16 | QR scan checks in **automatically** when booked; when not booked it goes to open spots. **AMENDED Sep 27 by #47:** scanning (or tapping Check in) *more than 15 minutes early* offers two one-tap "Start now" choices instead of checking in. | Fewest taps. | A confirm button after scanning. |
 | 17 | The kiosk handles **walk-in booking**, and its copy never mentions asking staff | Members can do everything on the tablet. Christie removed the Studio Tech wording. | "Book in the app first, then come back." |
 | 18 | **No live kiln status.** The standing schedule (Mon Member Firing: glaze & bisque firings; Wed unload; shelf cutoff 9:30 AM Monday) is listed in What's on; exceptions are posted as Changes; plus fun "Firing photos" posts | Keeping status current is a staff chore, and a stale status is worse than none. | A kiln status strip with loading/firing/cooling states. |
 | 19 | Kilns are referred to **by name only** (Hestia, Lumi) | The names are internal, but members will pick them up quickly. | Adding descriptions. |
 | 20 | Office Hours are **listed only**, with no RSVP and no "remind me" | They're drop-in. Christie's call. | RSVP or remind-me toggles. |
 | 21 | One-off events use **"I'm in"**, which doesn't count toward hours, and RSVPers are notified about changes | Lets staff reach exactly the people who planned to come. | — |
 | 22 | Pause and cancel requests go by **email** to clayhub@tinkerartstudio.com, and questions go to the **Ask Clay Hub** Google Form (link out) | Simplest option that works. No request flow to build or debug. | An in-app request form. |
 | 23 | **SUPERSEDED Sep 27 by #53.** ~~Membership Manager is the single source of truth, read directly, with no synced copy.~~ Membership Manager is **still** the single source of truth, and status still drives access automatically. But member data now reaches My Clay Hub through a **one-way projection** into its own project, because My Clay Hub lives in a separate Firebase project (#30). | Staff never update two systems (unchanged). | Syncing a separate copy of member data (now chosen, one-way only, because of #30). |
 | 24 | Full phone numbers never reach the members app or kiosk; the kiosk matches on the last 4 digits server-side. **Still stands.** Under #53 the projection carries `phoneLast4` only, worked out inside `tinker-hq-apps`. | Privacy. | Storing phones in the app. |
 | 25 | **SUPERSEDED Sep 27 by #31.** ~~Staff screens live in Tinker HQ.~~ Staff still use their **Tinker HQ login** and a Manage Team access box. But the staff screens are a `/staff` section of My Clay Hub, and the login is exchanged for a My Clay Hub session. | Reuses the existing, tested login (unchanged). Tinker HQ is a launcher that doesn't host app screens. | Separate staff accounts (still rejected: staff never get a second password). |
 | 26 | **Activity log is immutable and kept forever**; reports are computed from it | Reports, CSVs, and all-time history depend on it, and it's hard to add later. | Updating booking docs in place only. |
 | 27 | Closing time is a **per-weekday setting**. Tuesday is fixed at 11 PM because of the cleaners | Hours may move to midnight on other nights. | Hard-coded hours. |
 | 28 | Paused members can still see the Feed and What's on | Keeps them connected to the studio during the pause. | Full lockout. |
 | 29 | Firing listings never say **which kiln** runs glaze vs bisque | Kilns sometimes swap roles. | "Lumi: glaze · Hestia: bisque". |
 
 ## Foundation decisions (Sep 25–27, 2026)
 
 These come from the foundation plan (`~/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/clayhub-members-foundation.html`), where each has a D-number. That plan went through four independent review rounds.
 
 | # | Plan | Date | Decision | Why |
 |---|---|---|---|---|
 | 30 | D0 | Sep 26 | **A separate Firebase project, `my-clay-hub`, only for this app.** There are exactly two projects: `tinker-hq-apps` for every staff app, and `my-clay-hub` for this one. | Members are the first people outside staff to sign in. Keeping their accounts out of `tinker-hq-apps` means no internal rule can ever trust them by mistake. |
 | 31 | D1 | Sep 27 | Staff screens are a **`/staff` section of My Clay Hub**, opened from a Tinker HQ tile. Staff sign in with their Tinker HQ login, which a server function exchanges for a My Clay Hub session (`staff_{uid}`). Staff rules check the staff roster live on every request. **To remove someone's staff access, untick them (or make them inactive) in Manage Team.** Disabling their login alone doesn't do it. | Same login and roles as today. Access changes take effect within a minute. |
 | 32 | D2 | Sep 27 | Server code in two codebases, `clayhub-link` (in studio-hub, the link only) and `my-clay-hub/functions`. Both deploy only through a functions-aware guard. | The link has to run where Membership Manager's data lives. Everything else stays in this app's own project. |
 | 33 | D3 | Sep 27 | **Member sign-in is gated by a claim.** After a magic-link or Google sign-in, `claimMembership` finds exactly one live member with that verified email and a sign-in-eligible status, then sets the claim. Every member rule also checks the member's **live** status. No paid "blocking function". | Only real members get in. A pause or offboarding takes effect immediately, not at the next token refresh. |
 | 34 | D4 | Sep 27 | **A member's status is worked out by `deriveStatus`** from the dates, with Membership Manager's `stage` as a backstop. The rules are in a fixed order (DATA-MODEL.md), and the result is one of: active, paused, review, offboarded, not_yet, removed. Specific gaps give `review` (booking locked, feed still visible, staff flagged) instead of a lockout: a missing or unknown stage, a malformed last day, a pause with a missing or malformed date, `offboarding` with no last day, or `paused` with no pause dates. Every rule is in DATA-MODEL.md. The last access day itself still has access. | `stage` is only brought up to date when someone opens Membership Manager. Dates are the truth. |
 | 35 | D4a | Sep 27 | **A pause's end date is the last paused day.** Jan 2 – Feb 1 means back on Feb 2 ("Booking opens back up on Feb 2"). | Christie: "the day they can RETURN to the studio is Feb 2." |
 | 36 | D5 | Sep 27 | Add an accurate **`memberSince`** to Membership Manager. Christie fills in missing phones. Member-since shows on Profile and in the Reports member panel. | There's no reliable join date today. |
 | 37 | D6 | Sep 27 | **Nothing ever writes back to Membership Manager.** | Membership Manager re-saves whole records and would erase anything else. Any change to this would be a new, reviewed decision. |
 | 38 | D7 | Sep 27 | Plain collection names in `my-clay-hub` (`members`, `bookings`, `settings`, …). | The project holds only this app. |
 | 39 | D8 | Sep 27 | The front-door tablet signs in as its own `my-clay-hub` account (`kiosk:true`). It has no connection to the timeclock kiosks. | The timeclock kiosk can read staff records. This one mustn't. |
 | 40 | D9 | Sep 27 | A staff member who is also a Clay Hub member has two identities: a member login and a staff session. | They never mix. Regular staff only get the staff side if their box is ticked. |
 | 41 | D10 | Sep 27 | Clay Hub Booking's leftover data is sealed by removing its rules, snapshotted, then deleted by Christie. Only Booking-only login accounts are disabled. | It was never deployed, and it had a security hole. |
 | 42 | D11 | Sep 27 | **Permissions are narrow and one way:** dedicated service accounts; the link can read `tinker-hq-apps` but not write it; it can only *invoke* one private function in `my-clay-hub`; the ingest function can't delete. The full inventory is in the foundation plan. | Server code ignores Firestore rules, so it has to be contained by permissions instead. |
 | 43 | D12 | Sep 27 | The app is named **My Clay Hub**. Project ID `my-clay-hub` (fallbacks `myclayhub`, `my-clay-hub-app`). Manage Team key `my-clay-hub`. Repo `Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub`. | Christie's choice. |
 | 44 | D13 | Sep 27 | `my-clay-hub` backups are **server-side only**: point-in-time recovery, delete protection, a managed daily backup, and the vault export. It isn't added to `backup.js`. | `backup.js` depends on Christie's personal login. |
 | 45 | D14 | Sep 27 | **Keep the kiosk name list** (amends #9): members whose booking is live now (15 minutes before start through the end), grouped as "Here now" and "Arriving soon". First names, with a last initial only when two first names match (and the first two letters of the last name when the initials match too). No area. Display-only. | Christie: it's only seen inside the studio, and it builds community by matching names to faces. |
 | 46 | D15 | Sep 27 | **Staff no-show alerts in Phase 1**, including phone push for the two staff. They go out at last call (+20 min), for no-shows only, with "They're here: check in" or "Not here". | So Maryssa can check whether someone is actually there before their hours are released. |
 | 47 | D16 | Sep 27 | **"Start now"** (amends #16): checking in more than 15 minutes early offers "Start now, same length" or "Start now, keep my end time". The second one counts toward the cap. | Arriving early should be easy. |
 | 48 | D17 | Sep 27 | **15-minute times.** Start time is a dropdown. "How long" chips show the length and the end time ("3h / 9:00"), plus an "Other end time" option. The meter shows quarter hours. Hours returned on early check-out are rounded **up** to the next 15 minutes. | Members have always had 15-minute times in Skedda. Rounding up favours the member. |
 | 49 | D18 | Sep 27 | Booking opens **14 days** ahead (a setting). | Close to what members have now in Skedda, and still simple. |
 | 50 | D19 | Sep 27 | Staff with the `my-clay-hub` box ticked (Maryssa, the studio tech) get the Today view, check-in for others, posts, changes, events, **reports and CSVs**. **Settings** are for managers and admins only. | Maryssa runs reports herself. Settings change the rules for everyone. |
 | 51 | D20 | Sep 27 | **The Clay Hub brand**: the bright Clay Hub palette plus Tinker teal, Jost, the logo SVGs, flat-bottomed clay blobs, and pot icons for events. Readability comes first. See DESIGN.md. | Christie's brand sheet. Teal ties it to Tinker. |
 | 52 | D21 | Sep 27 | Every member gets a **permanent `memberId`** in Membership Manager, plus a "Change email" action that keeps it. The change is **one atomic batch**: the new doc (same `memberId`, live) and the old doc marked `retired: true`. It **refuses** if a live doc already exists for the new email. Doc IDs stay as they are. | Email-based IDs collide, and an email change used to leave a stale second record. |
 | 53 | D22 | Sep 27 | **The one-way link, "re-read, then send a snapshot"** (supersedes #23's "no copy"). When a member record changes, the link re-reads that member's current truth and sends a complete snapshot, stamped with when it was read. The newest snapshot always wins. Only name, email, status dates, member-since and `phoneLast4` are copied. | Correct even when changes arrive late, twice or out of order, and through deletes and email changes. |
 | 54 | D23 | Sep 27 | 2nd-generation Cloud Functions. Database location, trigger location and function region recorded separately. Retries explicitly on. Explicit `maxInstances`, one Admin app per instance, and an Artifact Registry cleanup policy. | 1st gen can't be upgraded in place later. Retries are off by default. |
 | 55 | — | Sep 27 | **No recurring bookings** (for example "every Tuesday 6–9") in My Clay Hub for now. Members book each session individually, within the 14-day window. | Christie: "let's start simpler without it." The old Clay Hub Booking notes said members used recurring bookings in Skedda, so this is a deliberate choice, not an oversight. It can be revisited later as its own decision. |
 | 56 | C6 | Sep 28 | **Every booking belongs wholly to its start date's week.** A booking is `[start, end)`; no booked minute is at or after that day's closing time; an end of 12:00 AM adds no minutes to the next day. A booking with any minute in the next day is malformed (no `weekKey`). Phase F's settings must reject a closing time later than 12:00 AM. | Phase D plan (Codex review): the data model gave each booking one `weekKey` but never said what a Monday-crossing booking does. SPEC §4–5 already rule overnight bookings out. |
 | 57 | C7 | Sep 28 | `weekKey` is **ISO-8601**, with the ISO week-year and a two-digit week (`2026-W09`; 2027-01-01 → `2026-W53`). | At a year boundary, two readings of "week" give different keys. |
 | 58 | C8 | Sep 28 | A pause whose end is before its start is **malformed**, so `deriveStatus` gives `review` (rule 7). | Otherwise it would fall through to `active` silently; `review` is how #34 handles every other data gap. |
 | 59 | — | Sep 28 | A pause with a start date but **no end date** gives **`review`**, not "paused indefinitely" (rule 7 as written). The member can sign in and see the feed but can't book; staff see a flag and add the end date, and the pause's booking cancellation happens then. | Christie chose it over the alternatives. The D-5 implementation review found Membership Manager's Quick Log can save a pause with a blank end, so this is a reachable state, not a typo. |
 | 60 | D2 | Oct 1 | `functions/iam-expectations.json` allows **exactly one** Editor holder: `760301318440@cloudservices.gserviceaccount.com` (the Google APIs Service Agent). Editor held by anyone else, a build account above all, is still a difference. | Google granted it Editor during the first functions deploy (when the CLI turned on the Firebase Extensions API). It's Google-managed, nothing we deploy runs as it, and Google advises against removing its grant. Christie chose to allow it rather than remove it. |
 | 61 | D2 | Oct 1 | **IAM readings only when needed** (replaces "after every deploy"). The functions guard records `attest_needed` and why: needed for an unverified deploy, the first deploy of a codebase, a changed function set, declarations, IAM expectations, `firebase.json` or `.firebaserc`, a Google API turned on during the deploy, or a reading still owed. Otherwise "not needed", naming the attestation it relies on. | Christie asked why each deploy needed so many Console screenshots. The automatic checks (hash, settings, the unauthenticated probe) still run every time. A role changed by hand in the Console isn't seen, so take a reading after one. |
 | 62 | D-4 | Oct 1 | **Scheduled functions in the functions guard** (F13 amended for schedules only; plan `my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export`, V2/V3). A function is declared `"trigger": "https"` or `"trigger": "schedule"`; a declaration's keys are exact. A schedule declares its schedule, `timeZone` `"UTC"` and all five retry values, and no invoker (the CLI makes the runtime account its only caller); the guard and the backstop refuse a manifest whose values differ or are unset. The CLI can't read a Cloud Scheduler job, so Christie reads it in the Console and `--attest` checks it (`scheduler_attested`; the complete job list = every DEPLOYED schedule, i.e. each codebase's newest attempt). **Exception to "every HTTP response sets `x-tinker-reached`":** scheduled functions, whose response firebase-functions writes itself; the unauthenticated probe must still be refused. | M6 required the guard to read the schedule back before D-4's function ships; the CLI never clears a retry setting removed from code, and can't see a job at all. Christie chose the header exception (O6). |
 | 63 | D-4 | Oct 2 | **The weekly vault export** (plan `my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export`, V1, V4–V12, O5). A second functions codebase, `vault`, separate from the canary: `vaultExport` (scheduled, Sundays 09:00 UTC, 3 retries about 10/20/40 min apart, 30-min timeout) copies the whole database to its own folder `weekly/<UTC date>-<6 chars>/` in bucket **`my-clay-hub-vault-exports`** in `tinker-hq-vault` (US multi-region, 56-day lifecycle, 7-day soft delete, no versioning, no retention lock), and waits for Google to finish, every call held to the run's 1,500 s budget; it succeeds only when the export is done, error-free, SUCCESSFUL, at the expected prefix, and its folder is complete. A retry finds a complete folder ("already done") or the running export ("resume") before it starts anything; a rare duplicate is a second complete copy in its own folder, never a mixed one (O5). `vaultFresh` answers a Monitoring uptime check: 200 while the newest complete copy is at most 7 d 18 h old (the email lands at about 8 days). Accounts: `vault-export@` (custom role `vaultExporter`: export + operations get/list; `vaultLister` on the bucket), `vault-watch@` (`vaultLister` on the bucket only), the Firestore service agent (`firestoreExportWriter` on the bucket only, granted in V-4). No deployed function or service account can import into production (only Christie, as Owner, by hand: DATA-RESTORE.md). Built on `@google-cloud/firestore-api` 0.2.0 (where Firestore 9.3.0's admin client and protos live) rather than `@google-cloud/firestore` as the plan first named. Restoring: DATA-RESTORE.md. | The project's own backups (PITR, daily backups) live inside the project that could fail; this is the off-project copy that gates real member data (Phase E). Christie chose US, the uptime check and the 7 d 18 h threshold (O2, O4), a rare duplicate over a lock (O5), and a restore rehearsal after Phase E (O3). |
-| 64 | D-4 | Oct 7 | **A Force run is keyed by the day it actually runs.** V-4's failure-first Force run (Wed Oct 7) showed Cloud Scheduler sends a Force run the job's *next* scheduled time (Sunday Oct 11 09:00 UTC), not "now" as the V7 tests assumed, so a weekday catch-up run would have taken the coming Sunday's key and that Sunday's real run would have skipped as "already done". `runKey` now keys a scheduled time still in the future by today's UTC date; a real run and its retries (J15) keep their scheduled date. Until this ships: no Force run at all (on any day, including a Sunday after 09:00 UTC, it would take a later Sunday's key), and V-4's first real export is Sunday Oct 11's scheduled run. | Christie chose it (fix next, before Phase E; don't hold V-4)
+| 64 | D-4 | Oct 7 | **A Force run is keyed by the day it actually runs.** V-4's failure-first Force run (Wed Oct 7) showed Cloud Scheduler sends a Force run the job's *next* scheduled time (Sunday Oct 11 09:00 UTC), not "now" as the V7 tests assumed, so a weekday catch-up run would have taken the coming Sunday's key and that Sunday's real run would have skipped as "already done". `runKey` now keys a scheduled time still in the future by today's UTC date; a real run and its retries (J15) keep their scheduled date. Accepted limit (impl review, Codex): the header can't tell a Force run's retry from a new Force run, so a retry crossing UTC midnight is keyed by the new day and makes at most one extra complete copy in its own folder (O5); if that day is Sunday, the copy is genuinely Sunday's and the 09:00 run's "already done" is correct. Runbook: start Force runs before 20:00 UTC. Until this ships: no Force run at all (on any day, including a Sunday after 09:00 UTC, it would take a later Sunday's key), and V-4's first real export is Sunday Oct 11's scheduled run. | Christie chose it (fix next, before Phase E; don't hold V-4)
diff --git a/tests/functions/vault/export-run.test.js b/tests/functions/vault/export-run.test.js
index 4199c71..daab793 100644
--- a/tests/functions/vault/export-run.test.js
+++ b/tests/functions/vault/export-run.test.js
@@ -288,80 +288,100 @@ test('a hung operation read late in the wait is bounded by what is left, never p
 });
 
 test('the client is told the time left too, so it stops retrying at the deadline', async () => {
   const { a, run } = setup();
   const seen = [];
   const { exportDocuments, getOperation, listOperationsAsync } = a;
   a.exportDocuments = (req, opts) => { seen.push(['export', opts]); return exportDocuments(req, opts); };
   a.getOperation = (req, opts) => { seen.push(['get', opts]); return getOperation(req, opts); };
   a.listOperationsAsync = (req, opts) => { seen.push(['list', opts]); return listOperationsAsync(req, opts); };
   await run();
   assert.deepEqual([...new Set(seen.map(([what]) => what))].sort(), ['export', 'get', 'list']);
   for (const [what, opts] of seen) assert.ok(opts && opts.timeout > 0 && opts.timeout <= C.CALL_TIMEOUT_MS, `${what}: ${JSON.stringify(opts)}`);
 });
 
 test('with less than 5 minutes of budget left after the reads, no export is started (the next retry starts it)', async () => {
   const { a, b, k, run } = setup();
   const getFiles = b.getFiles.bind(b);
   b.getFiles = async (q) => { k.advance(C.WAIT_BUDGET_MS - C.START_RESERVE_MS + 1000); return getFiles(q); };   // a slow listing
   await assert.rejects(run(), /only \d+ s of the budget left, too little to start an export; the next retry starts it/);
   assert.equal(a.exports.length, 0);
 });
 
 test('a fresh suffix never repeats a folder or an operation prefix already seen for the date', async () => {
   const { a, b, run } = setup();
   b.add(`weekly/${KEY}-aaaaaa/all_namespaces/all_kinds/output-0`, '2026-10-04T09:00:00Z');   // an incomplete folder
   a.seed({ prefix: gs(`weekly/${KEY}-bbbbbb`), done: true, outcome: 'FAILED', error: { code: 7, message: 'denied' } });
   const r = await run();
   assert.equal(r.prefix, `weekly/${KEY}-cccccc/`);
   assert.equal(a.exports[0].outputUriPrefix, gs(`weekly/${KEY}-cccccc`));
 });
 
 test('an export that finishes between the folder listing and the operation listing → "already done", nothing started', async () => {
   const { a, b, run } = setup();
   const name = a.seed({ prefix: gs(`weekly/${KEY}-justin`), done: true, outcome: 'SUCCESSFUL' });
   const getFiles = b.getFiles.bind(b);
   let first = true;
   b.getFiles = async (q) => {
     const r = await getFiles(q);
     if (first && q.delimiter) { first = false; b.addExport(`weekly/${KEY}-justin/`); }   // it lands just after step 1
     return r;
   };
   const r = await run();
   assert.deepEqual(r, { key: KEY, decision: 'already done', prefix: `weekly/${KEY}-justin/`, operation: name });
   assert.equal(a.exports.length, 0);
 });
 
 test('the run key is the UTC date of the scheduled time — across a year end, and from "now" when the header is missing', () => {
   const LATER = Date.parse('2027-02-01T00:00:00Z');   // a real run and its retries arrive at or after their scheduled time
   assert.equal(runKey('2026-12-31T23:59:59.999Z', LATER), '2026-12-31');
   assert.equal(runKey('2027-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', LATER), '2027-01-01');
   assert.equal(runKey('2027-01-03T02:00:00-07:00', LATER), '2027-01-03', 'a Denver-offset time is still its UTC date');
   assert.equal(runKey(SUNDAY, Date.parse(SUNDAY)), KEY, 'exactly on time is the scheduled date');
   assert.equal(runKey(SUNDAY, Date.parse('2026-10-04T09:40:00Z')), KEY, 'a retry 40 min later keeps Sunday');
   assert.equal(runKey(undefined, Date.parse('2027-01-01T00:00:01Z')), '2027-01-01');
   assert.equal(runKey('', Date.parse('2026-12-31T23:59:00Z')), '2026-12-31');
   assert.throws(() => runKey('not a time', 0), /is not a time/);
   assert.equal(snapshotTimeMs(Date.parse('2027-01-01T00:00:30.500Z')), Date.parse('2026-12-31T23:59:00Z'));
 });
 
 test('a run with the header missing uses the clock for its key', async () => {
   const { a, run } = setup({ start: '2026-10-07T15:30:00Z' });
   const r = await run(undefined);
   assert.equal(r.key, '2026-10-07');
   assert.equal(a.exports[0].outputUriPrefix, gs('weekly/2026-10-07-aaaaaa'));
 });
 
 test('a Force run carries the NEXT scheduled time (observed Oct 7 2026) and is keyed by the day it actually runs', async () => {
   // A Wednesday Force run arrived with scheduleTime = the coming Sunday 09:00 UTC.
   assert.equal(runKey('2026-10-11T02:00:00-07:00', Date.parse('2026-10-07T18:54:31Z')), '2026-10-07');
   // On Sunday before 09:00 the next scheduled time is the same day; after 09:00 it is next Sunday. Both key to today.
   assert.equal(runKey('2026-10-11T09:00:00Z', Date.parse('2026-10-11T06:00:00Z')), '2026-10-11');
   assert.equal(runKey('2026-10-18T09:00:00Z', Date.parse('2026-10-11T15:00:00Z')), '2026-10-11');
   // Late on a Saturday (UTC), still Saturday's key — never the coming Sunday's.
   assert.equal(runKey('2026-10-11T09:00:00Z', Date.parse('2026-10-10T23:59:59Z')), '2026-10-10');
 
   const { a, run } = setup({ start: '2026-10-07T18:54:31Z' });
   const r = await run('2026-10-11T09:00:00Z');
   assert.equal(r.key, '2026-10-07');
   assert.equal(a.exports[0].outputUriPrefix, gs('weekly/2026-10-07-aaaaaa'), "not Sunday's key, so Sunday's real run still exports");
 });
+
+test('a Force-run retry that crosses UTC midnight takes the new day\'s key: at most one extra complete copy, each in its own folder (impl review, Codex)', async () => {
+  // Saturday 23:50Z: a Force run (header = Sunday 09:00) started an export that is still running when the attempt ended.
+  const { a, k, run } = setup({ start: '2026-10-11T00:10:00Z' });
+  const saturday = a.seed({ prefix: gs('weekly/2026-10-10-satrun'), pollsToFinish: 10_000 });
+  // Its retry, after midnight, carries the same header but is keyed Sunday, so it neither sees nor resumes Saturday's
+  // export and starts its own: a second complete copy (O5's accepted duplicate), never a shared or mixed folder.
+  const r = await run('2026-10-11T09:00:00Z');
+  assert.equal(r.key, '2026-10-11');
+  assert.equal(r.decision, 'exported');
+  assert.equal(a.exports.length, 1);
+  assert.equal(a.exports[0].outputUriPrefix, gs('weekly/2026-10-11-aaaaaa'));
+  assert.notEqual(r.operation, saturday);
+  // Sunday's real 09:00 run then finds that genuinely-Sunday copy complete: "already done", no third export.
+  k.advance(Date.parse('2026-10-11T09:00:05Z') - k.now());
+  const sunday = await run('2026-10-11T09:00:00Z');
+  assert.equal(sunday.key, '2026-10-11');
+  assert.equal(sunday.decision, 'already done');
+  assert.equal(a.exports.length, 1);
+});

